<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="NADH repair (eukaryotes)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-6938" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; Studies in the 1950s have shown that |FRAME: GAPOXNPHOSPHN-RXN &quot;glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase&quot;| slowly catalyzes the formation of a hydrated form of NADH, called |FRAME: NADHX|, from NADH |CITS: [Rafter54]|. The equilibrium of this hydration reaction is in favor (100/1) of the hydrated form |CITS: [3061454]|, making the reaction virtually irreversible. In addition, NADHX can be formed spontaneously from NADH under slightly acidic conditions. NADHX and NADPHX are inhibitors of several dehydrogenases |CITS: [ 9851831]| and it is thus important to eliminate them. This is achieved by the enzyme |FRAME: ENSG00000153481-MONOMER| which has been originally described from yeast |CITS: [Meinhart56]|. Upon identification of the yeast enzyme it was found that homologs are found in the overwhelming majority of eukaryotes and prokaryotes. The human and yeast enzymes also accept (S)-NADPHX, with higher maximal activity |CITS: [21994945]|. Since both |FRAME: CPD0-2472| and |FRAME: CPD-653| are formed, but |FRAME: ENSG00000153481-MONOMER| can only accept the &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-form, the &lt;i&gt;R&lt;/i&gt; form needs to be epimerized to the &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-form before it can converted back to NADH. This is achieved by the enzyme |FRAME: ENSG00000163382-MONOMER| |CITS: [21994945]|. The two enzymes are encoded by two different genes in yeast and humans. Many prokaryotes possess an ADP-dependent bifunctional enzyme that catalyzes both reactions |CITS: [21994945]| (see |FRAME: PWY-6938-1|).</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="zhang" />
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
  </Authors>
